WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.120
The first film showed how chromosome copies reach matching nuclei.

2
00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:09.580
How can a frozen onion root reveal which part of that cycle takes longest?

3
00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.600
The cells cannot answer, but their patterns can.

4
00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:18.850
A stained root tip is a snapshot.

5
00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:22.630
Each cell is caught at one moment of its cycle.

6
00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:26.730
Some show loose chromosomes inside a yellow nucleus.

7
00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:33.050
Others show chromosomes coiling, lining up, separating, or reaching opposite ends.

8
00:00:34.083 --> 00:00:38.263
Imagine many runners photographed at random points around a track.

9
00:00:39.503 --> 00:00:43.723
A slow section holds more runners because each runner stays there longer.

10
00:00:45.403 --> 00:00:47.543
Cycling cells follow the same counting model.

11
00:00:48.703 --> 00:00:50.923
A longer phase should appear in more cells.

12
00:00:51.967 --> 00:00:55.427
Two phase lanes now contain different numbers of cells.

13
00:00:57.067 --> 00:01:00.487
Suppose every cycling cell moves steadily and the sample is fair.

14
00:01:01.847 --> 00:01:04.207
Which lane represents the longer-lasting phase?

15
00:01:05.607 --> 00:01:06.607
Hold your answer.

16
00:01:12.383 --> 00:01:15.103
The fuller lane represents the longer phase.

17
00:01:16.423 --> 00:01:19.823
More cells were caught there because each one remains there longer.

18
00:01:21.083 --> 00:01:23.443
This gives a fraction, not a stopwatch reading.

19
00:01:24.963 --> 00:01:29.503
Divide the cells in one phase by all the cells counted, assuming every one is

20
00:01:29.503 --> 00:01:30.503
cycling.

21
00:01:30.967 --> 00:01:33.727
Now sort one teaching set by visible clues.

22
00:01:35.127 --> 00:01:36.867
Intact nuclei go to interphase.

23
00:01:38.267 --> 00:01:39.927
Coiled chromosomes go to prophase.

24
00:01:41.287 --> 00:01:42.887
A middle row marks metaphase.

25
00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:46.067
Separating groups mark anaphase.

26
00:01:47.447 --> 00:01:49.287
Two forming nuclei mark telophase.

27
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:55.200
To estimate time, multiply a phase's fraction by the total cycle time.

28
00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:01.220
The teaching activity supplies a round total time rather than measuring it.

29
00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:03.680
Put that value on screen.

30
00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.460
The result is a model estimate for this set.

31
00:02:08.433 --> 00:02:12.713
The model can fail if the pictures were chosen to display every stage.

32
00:02:13.993 --> 00:02:17.273
It also weakens if cells stopped cycling or changed pace.

33
00:02:18.633 --> 00:02:23.633
A random field, a clear sampling rule, and a measured total time would support

34
00:02:23.633 --> 00:02:24.653
stronger estimates.

35
00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:27.547
One more distinction matters.

36
00:02:29.007 --> 00:02:34.347
Mitosis separates chromosomes between nuclei, while cytokinesis separates the cell.

37
00:02:35.787 --> 00:02:40.287
Root-tip photographs can show both nearby, but the phase count sorts chromosome

38
00:02:40.287 --> 00:02:45.287
patterns. It does not directly time membrane or wall completion.

39
00:02:46.547 --> 00:02:50.187
The two processes overlap, so one snapshot can catch both.

40
00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:53.790
The count still teaches an important idea.

41
00:02:55.330 --> 00:03:00.550
Cells spend time growing, copying DNA, and preparing before chromosomes visibly

42
00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:04.910
separate. That preparation serves the first film's rule.

43
00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:10.730
Copy each chromosome first; separate the copies so each nucleus gets one.

44
00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:13.790
And the counting rule is just as short.

45
00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:18.210
In a fair snapshot, a longer phase holds more cells.
